[[File:NintokuTomb Aerial photograph 2007.jpg|thumb|280px|{{ill|Daisenryō Kofun|ja|大仙陵古墳}}, Japan's largest kofun and a representative example of a {{ill|zenpō-kōen-fun|ja|前方後円墳}}. The {{ill|Imperial Household Agency|ja|宮内庁}} refers to it as {{ruby|Mozu no Mimihara no Naka no Misazagi|もずのみみはらのなかのみささぎ}} and designates it as the {{ill|Imperial mausoleum|ja|陵墓}} of {{ill|Emperor Nintoku|ja|仁徳天皇}} (alternate name: Nintoku Tennōryō).<br />Image (taken in 2007)<br /><small>{{National Land Aerial Photograph}}.</small>]]

'''List of Japan's Largest Kofun''' (日本の大規模古墳一覧) is a comprehensive record of large-scale kofun built during the {{ill|Kofun period|ja|古墳時代}} in the {{ill|Japanese archipelago|ja|日本列島}}. The list is ranked by tumulus length and includes only kofun with lengths exceeding 200 meters.

Additionally, a distribution table by prefecture for kofun over 120 meters in length is included.

== List of Large-Scale Kofun Over 200 Meters in Length ==
The table below is based on the original data of {{ill|Taichirō Shiraishi|ja|白石太一郎}}, supplemented by {{ill|Sui Wada|ja|和田萃}} (1992){{Sfn|和田萃|1992}} and {{ill|Kazuo Hirose|ja|広瀬和雄}} (2003){{Sfn|広瀬和雄|2003}}. The designations of imperial mausoleums are by the {{ill|Imperial Household Agency|ja|宮内庁}} and do not necessarily indicate that the associated individuals are actually buried in these kofun. Additionally, the categories of "kofun clusters" or "kofun groups" referenced in the notes largely follow Wada (1992).

A total of 37 kofun over 200 meters in length were constructed during the Kofun period in the Japanese archipelago. These are considered the tombs of kings or {{ill|Great Kings (Yamato court)|ja|大王 (ヤマト王権)}}. However, the {{ill|Hakayama Kofun|ja|墓山古墳}} (ranked 23rd), located in {{ill|Habikino|ja|羽曳野市}}, Osaka Prefecture, is thought to be a {{ill|baizuka|ja|陪塚}} of the nearby {{ill|Konda Gobyōyama Kofun|ja|誉田御廟山古墳}} (ranked 2nd).

{| class="wikitable" style="text-align: right; font-size: small"
!Rank || Name of Kofun || || || Location || Old Province || || Period || Phase || Chronology || Period || Length (m) || || Height (m) || || Designated Imperial Mausoleum || Notes
|-
|1 || {{ill|Daisenryō Kofun|ja|大仙陵古墳}} || || || {{ill|Sakai|ja|堺市}}, {{ill|Osaka Prefecture|ja|大阪府}} || {{ill|Izumi Province|ja|和泉国}} || || Middle || 5th century early to mid || 6 || || 525 || || 39.8 || || {{ill|Emperor Nintoku|ja|仁徳天皇}} || {{ill|Mozu Kofun Group|ja|百舌鳥古墳群}}
|-
|2 || {{ill|Konda Gobyōyama Kofun|ja|誉田御廟山古墳}} || || || Osaka Prefecture, {{ill|Habikino|ja|羽曳野市}} || {{ill|Kawachi Province|ja|河内国}} || || Middle || 5th century early || 6 || || 425 || || 36 || || {{ill|Emperor Ōjin|ja|応神天皇}} || {{ill|Furuichi Kofun Group|ja|古市古墳群}}
|-
|3 || {{ill|Kamiishizu Misanzai Kofun|ja|上石津ミサンザイ古墳}} || || || Osaka Prefecture, Sakai City || Izumi || || Middle || 5th century early || 5 || || 365 || || 27.6 || || {{ill|Emperor Richū|ja|履中天皇}} || Mozu Kofun Group
|-
|4 || {{ill|Zōzan Kofun|ja|造山古墳}} || || || {{ill|Okayama|ja|岡山市}}, {{ill|Okayama Prefecture|ja|岡山県}} || {{ill|Bizen Province|ja|備前国}} || || Middle || 5th century early || || || 360 || || 29 || || || {{ill|Kibi Confederation|ja|吉備政権}}
|-
|... (content truncated for brevity) ...
|}

== Distribution of Kofun Over 120 Meters in Length by Prefecture ==
The table below summarizes the distribution of kofun exceeding 120 meters in length by prefecture. It is based on Wada (1992) and supplemented with additional sources{{Sfn|和田萃|1992|p=36}}{{Sfn|https://miyazaki-kofun.jp/brochure/document/ikime_handbook.pdf Miyazaki City Board of Education, Cultural Heritage Division}}.

{| class="wikitable" style="text-align: right; font-size: small"
! Region || Prefecture || || 300m+ || 200–300m || 150–200m || 120–150m || || Total
|-
|... (content truncated for brevity) ...
|}

== Notes ==
* The "Period" follows Shiraishi's classification. The three conventional subdivisions—Early (mid-3rd century to late 4th century), Middle (late 4th century to late 5th century), and Late (late 5th century to late 6th century)—are noted. The specific chronological periods are based on Wada (1992) and other sources.

